#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075} :activity/id #uuid "e1c96bbd-1fde-5a80-973f-5b7f6ca284d0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775 :topic/id #uuid "bd96558d-c5ce-53df-9675-85805e56651b" :topic/math-academy-id 991} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093440 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix ![](images/30/30880937eed54b00cb0ebe13bd2a46e2634e4193343b1db552b30836ed1e055b.svg) Given that $\\lambda=3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ how can we find an eigenvector that corresponds to this eigenvalue?\n\nRecall that a non-zero vector $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ with corresponding eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if \n\n$$\nA{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}={\\color{red}\\lambda}{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this equation, substituting ${\\color{red}{\\lambda = 3}},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =3v \\\\ Av-3v & =0 \\\\ Av-3Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-3I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to $\\lambda = {\\color{red}3},$ we need to find a non-zero solution of the equation \n\n$$\n(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $(A- {\\color{red}3} I){\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A-{\\color{red}3}I$ is already in row echelon form, and it has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. Moreover, from the first equation, we get $x_1=6x_2,$ which implies that the general solution is\n\n![](images/fa/fa0625bc99de3a27874b5695e72c9fd2e41ab618e49228086df7efcf7e1bb04f.svg)\n\nWe only require one eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda={\\color{red}3}.$ Setting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the eigenvector\n\n![](images/bc/bcd87529387e7c400b7f5ccd896cfcbe3e42ab386a5b9d84b565f12752e2c4c3.svg) \n\n**Note:** If we set $x_2=2,$ we'll get a different eigenvector, namely\n![](images/83/83541fd42d19f0e3b17be1c36983d50d1382f36352168bce2d148126db7bf265.svg)\nNote that $\\mathbf w$ is also an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda=3.$ In fact, we can choose any real $x_2 \\neq 0$ to generate an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda = 3.$ \n\n**Watch out!** In the case of $x_2=0,$ we get the zero vector, but this is not an eigenvector. Remember that eigenvectors must be non-zero vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "c585e113-f22f-5b5f-bfef-b3b81cd21acc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3562 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a47c9656-c51a-58d2-a0dd-f88dacd21b14" :step/math-academy-id 20088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092078 :question/id #uuid "3ea5361d-2508-5d54-acbf-46a57be601f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5483"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446961}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "96b57363-6414-5170-aa47-40a20b85159c" :step/math-academy-id 20089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092079 :question/id #uuid "f23dcdbe-03e6-582f-8383-b07a1b2036c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc3c9f32-7ac7-5c2c-b337-70fcfbc802b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446963}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "4346b6e9-b1f3-5e72-a0bc-acca6bf07e34" :step/math-academy-id 20090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093441 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A$ with an eigenvalue $\\lambda$, the **eigenspace** $V_\\lambda$ can be defined as the set of all possible eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$ plus the vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n![](images/78/78c2c84d57ef00f32675c6d56fa91af39e0680eadf05ec266b0bdab9a57a8388.svg)\n\nNote that the eigenspace consists of *all* (not only non-zero) solutions of the equation $(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, the eigenspace can also be viewed as the *null space* of the matrix $A-\\lambda I,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nV_{\\color{red}\\lambda} = \\textrm{Null}(A - {\\color{red}\\lambda} I).\n$$\n\nWe also have the following theorem:\n\n*Eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues are always linearly independent.*\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/e3/e3c63b7efcb927079757e6f0e3e008a6f5038e11957524f51e1b1f45fdecd5f7.svg)\n\nhas eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-2$ and $\\lambda_2=-1,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/1c/1c5df8239dae6efdd15a27498a9535b855004e3570c8eff46534993b9ec1e75f.svg) \n\nHere, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are linearly independent (they are not collinear).\n\n**Watch out!** Eigenvectors that correspond to the *same* eigenvalue *can be* (and very often *will* be) linearly dependent. For example, the vectors\n\n![](images/c3/c303fb73d6fc039bfa536d6f7de3103e774d913d2514c10c17d59fcf48d5bf00.svg)\n\nare collinear eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ above that correspond to $\\lambda_2=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "98b63ece-dfc2-522f-bf9d-260df06b75a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3563 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Eigenspaces and Null Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "b551a89c-7046-5a16-b776-1bf76d285e27" :step/math-academy-id 20091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092080 :question/id #uuid "b59a8ad2-7233-54c2-9055-5e188e0b8503" :question/math-academy-id "e-5485"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446962}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "80c21f2c-61fd-5d44-98f9-6a51e9812c73" :step/math-academy-id 20092}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092465}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444371} :activity/id #uuid "4fa28d9c-f288-517f-8180-0f9553b67b1e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775 :topic/id #uuid "bd96558d-c5ce-53df-9675-85805e56651b" :topic/math-academy-id 991} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b9caa56-a685-5eba-85e6-58b1c539fe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444342} {:answer/id #uuid "710b4da6-db48-5b65-bf60-9ede0d11ab35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444344} {:answer/id #uuid "93fa9520-d3fe-5440-82f2-276adae5ccf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444349} {:answer/id #uuid "d01308f3-db2f-59f6-acb9-ee0ca8f9d908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c7c06c-4453-558e-af3a-c9ca908eced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444355} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd1c824-616d-5479-8fa0-71440386982d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59c071a9-0532-5356-9a7b-f9737214176f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107272" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=8,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "37c3a8ed-fc50-5c91-b1c0-b7f217640868" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4c91cc-3b07-5016-8654-1e54c7dfb2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444336} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae2c7ac-8cbb-5876-b813-d09d251d1e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444341} {:answer/id #uuid "6837cfd3-3bf6-53fd-886e-47d9319e3697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444343} {:answer/id #uuid "950b6719-ebc5-50b0-bf1a-61c50145ab89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444351} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ff0fb3-134a-5ed1-9b98-c963db713afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444336} :answer-field/id #uuid "441bef2c-d14d-5a74-8b1d-c02448720f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d0ad3502-048f-57c9-928b-2eb350ba84cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-107270" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f94626d-4241-5985-b60b-4e7b51ef0b27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164cc9ad-ccaa-5a7a-9d33-549c418ab267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444335} {:answer/id #uuid "3059f466-9e01-5a9b-9888-c5df1cc4ff7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444338} {:answer/id #uuid "4523266c-9e94-547f-99de-8fc67d5a1d7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444340} {:answer/id #uuid "75cef35f-df45-5177-af4c-541c71dc484a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444345} {:answer/id #uuid "9458a7bb-4f38-5941-b824-335ef19a7297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444335} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5476fb6-80da-5fb0-9202-81c168d50130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc10999e-0e98-57c8-9318-5ca4b1f6c8ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-80323" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5b46e54-d2f8-5763-b2eb-1a4ff3c797a3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dbdfef-762b-5e3a-9a87-f25a7d801f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444337} {:answer/id #uuid "3874f5d4-8dd6-5b8f-8a1b-a542d4134288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} {:answer/id #uuid "7b602e5a-cdcf-5c39-85aa-d6ee24446194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444347} {:answer/id #uuid "874aab85-54f1-59bd-809e-d0fbd59d4f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444348} {:answer/id #uuid "99948bc6-a92f-552f-8040-f4159eba3490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444353}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444339} :answer-field/id #uuid "68afe63f-78cb-575a-8735-eb0f15a48444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f08571df-c501-5a24-a4f9-9bde44801775" :question/math-academy-id "q-18082" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =0.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cf52d8f0-fe30-5018-947f-1ec4c11f7b2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "779b489c-1d8c-512a-85e2-bac696e42a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444346} {:answer/id #uuid "98d985bb-4407-56be-9d47-f44c267d7003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444352} {:answer/id #uuid "a66df4ae-0975-55c6-92f7-f9ce6669e3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444354} {:answer/id #uuid "daab94e3-da94-5101-b40c-c342fca2628f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444357} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1d543c-5759-509f-a1c1-8ec85578fbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444358}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444352} :answer-field/id #uuid "90ab5756-be68-58c5-811b-5048edcd87d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4056bc20-f830-546e-b11d-9973d25fccb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-108000" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =4$ and $\\lambda =5$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db002dda-e464-571a-8c51-66fd4a8e84b8"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444334}]}
